WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:01:10.799 --> 00:01:14.960
Here's an idea of just how much pricier

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:17.119
the basics have become.

00:01:14.960 --> 00:01:19.600
According to the UN's global Food Price

00:01:17.119 --> 00:01:22.799
Index, in the last year the cost of

00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:24.880
cereals went up almost 13%. 

00:01:22.799 --> 00:01:27.520
Meat 17%.

00:01:24.880 --> 00:01:30.000
Sugar is up 20% and vegetable

00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:32.720
oil shot up 34%. 

00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:51.119
Now there's a lot that goes into the

00:01:47.840 --> 00:01:53.600
price of food. Take, for example, a tomato.

00:01:51.119 --> 00:01:56.640
To grow one farmers will usually use

00:01:53.600 --> 00:01:59.680
fertiliser and pesticides. To harvest

00:01:56.640 --> 00:02:01.759
they need workers, equipment and fuel.

00:01:59.680 --> 00:02:03.920
And then the tomato needs transporting

00:02:01.759 --> 00:02:06.240
to wherever it's being sold.

00:02:03.920 --> 00:02:09.599
With processed food—say something like a

00:02:06.240 --> 00:02:11.520
frozen pizza—there's even more going on.

00:02:09.599 --> 00:02:12.879
Ingredients might need importing from

00:02:11.520 --> 00:02:15.280
around the world.

00:02:12.879 --> 00:02:17.440
Plus, it needs to be made, packaged and

00:02:15.280 --> 00:02:20.560
distributed. 

00:02:24.800 --> 00:02:28.560
Now what's been

00:02:26.480 --> 00:02:31.120
happening over the last year or so is

00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:32.480
that all those costs that go into food

00:02:31.120 --> 00:02:34.959
have been going up.

00:02:32.480 --> 00:02:37.040
It's inflation—price hikes across the

00:02:34.959 --> 00:02:39.440
board that feed into each other.

00:02:44.800 --> 00:02:48.640
So, you've got this backdrop of

00:02:46.720 --> 00:02:50.959
rising inflation, and then when it comes

00:02:48.640 --> 00:02:53.440
to food prices, there are some other big

00:02:50.959 --> 00:02:56.800
things going on right now, too. Let's

00:02:53.440 --> 00:02:56.800
start with supply chains.

00:02:56.959 --> 00:03:00.959
We're basically talking about all the

00:02:58.640 --> 00:03:02.239
cogs in the system of production,

00:03:00.959 --> 00:03:05.280
distribution,

00:03:02.239 --> 00:03:08.080
transport and retail. But right now the

00:03:05.280 --> 00:03:10.480
global supply chain is a mess, mostly

00:03:08.080 --> 00:03:13.360
because of the pandemic, which caused a

00:03:10.480 --> 00:03:15.200
big and sudden shift in production and

00:03:13.360 --> 00:03:17.280
consumption patterns.

00:03:15.200 --> 00:03:19.519
Then, when COVID lockdowns lifted, there

00:03:17.280 --> 00:03:20.480
was a surge in demand for all kinds of

00:03:19.519 --> 00:03:22.640
things.

00:03:20.480 --> 00:03:25.120
Right now the shipping industry is maxed

00:03:22.640 --> 00:03:26.879
out, and there's a shortage of containers.

00:03:25.120 --> 00:03:29.040
When it comes to food, it's causing

00:03:26.879 --> 00:03:30.959
delays in transporting bulk products

00:03:29.040 --> 00:03:32.879
like grains and sugar.

00:03:30.959 --> 00:03:35.920
All those delays, problems and

00:03:32.879 --> 00:03:38.959
bottlenecks are affecting supplies and

00:03:35.920 --> 00:03:38.959
pushing prices up.

00:03:48.400 --> 00:03:53.519
Remember, too, that there are

00:03:51.280 --> 00:03:55.840
people involved at every stage of the

00:03:53.519 --> 00:03:57.439
food supply chain who were also affected

00:03:55.840 --> 00:03:59.439
by the pandemic.

00:03:57.439 --> 00:04:02.239
A lot of people couldn't work because

00:03:59.439 --> 00:04:04.640
they had COVID, or were isolating.

00:04:02.239 --> 00:04:07.680
Some workers were sacked, others quit,

00:04:04.640 --> 00:04:09.120
switched jobs or retired early. Some were

00:04:07.680 --> 00:04:10.080
locked out of countries where they

00:04:09.120 --> 00:04:12.480
worked.

00:04:10.080 --> 00:04:15.120
Take the palm oil industry in Malaysia

00:04:12.480 --> 00:04:17.919
as just one example. 

00:04:27.600 --> 00:04:32.320
OK,so those are the main

00:04:29.680 --> 00:04:36.160
supply chain problems. Then there's

00:04:32.320 --> 00:04:38.800
energy prices. They've gone up, too,

00:04:36.160 --> 00:04:40.800
especially the cost of oil and gas.

00:04:38.800 --> 00:04:43.199
And that affects the food industry in

00:04:40.800 --> 00:04:45.280
lots of ways. To start with, most

00:04:43.199 --> 00:04:47.360
fertilisers rely on chemicals like

00:04:45.280 --> 00:04:50.400
nitrogen and ammonia and they're made

00:04:47.360 --> 00:04:53.040
with natural gas, which now costs more.

00:04:50.400 --> 00:04:55.520
Higher fuel prices mean getting anything

00:04:53.040 --> 00:04:57.440
anywhere is more expensive, and that cost

00:04:55.520 --> 00:05:00.000
increase is usually passed on to

00:04:57.440 --> 00:05:02.720
consumers. Another side effect of high

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:04.800
energy prices is that some farmers—

00:05:02.720 --> 00:05:06.720
especially in countries like the US,

00:05:04.800 --> 00:05:09.520
Brazil and Germany—have a bigger

00:05:06.720 --> 00:05:11.199
incentive to shift from food production

00:05:09.520 --> 00:05:13.840
to fuel production.

00:05:23.680 --> 00:05:28.800
Then, on top of all of

00:05:26.479 --> 00:05:31.680
this, you've got bad weather. Things like

00:05:28.800 --> 00:05:33.759
droughts, floods and extreme temperatures.

00:05:31.680 --> 00:05:36.400
Climate change is a big part of it. 

00:06:17.120 --> 00:06:21.520
So those are some of the more universal

00:06:19.039 --> 00:06:24.479
factors affecting food prices. But each

00:06:21.520 --> 00:06:26.160
country also has its own set of issues,

00:06:24.479 --> 00:06:27.840
like the state of its economy and what

00:06:26.160 --> 00:06:30.639
the government may be doing in the food

00:06:27.840 --> 00:06:32.840
sector. Things like taxes on food imports

00:06:30.639 --> 00:06:35.759
or subsidies to keep prices down for

00:06:32.840 --> 00:06:37.680
consumers. In Turkey, for example, people

00:06:35.759 --> 00:06:38.800
are dealing with a seriously weak

00:06:37.680 --> 00:06:41.360
currency. 

00:06:38.800 --> 00:06:43.199
Last year the lira lost 40% of

00:06:41.360 --> 00:06:45.280
its value against the dollar.

00:06:43.199 --> 00:06:48.240
That means anything that Turkey imports

00:06:45.280 --> 00:06:50.960
is way more expensive, including food and

00:06:48.240 --> 00:06:52.319
things used to produce food. 

00:07:14.160 --> 00:07:18.319
Turkey is just one

00:07:16.400 --> 00:07:20.400
example where things are particularly

00:07:18.319 --> 00:07:22.319
tough right now. But the poorest being

00:07:20.400 --> 00:07:24.319
hit the hardest, well, that applies

00:07:22.319 --> 00:07:26.880
everywhere because in general people

00:07:24.319 --> 00:07:29.840
with lower incomes spend a much bigger

00:07:26.880 --> 00:07:31.840
proportion of their income on food. And

00:07:29.840 --> 00:07:34.960
when something as essential as food

00:07:31.840 --> 00:07:36.800
suddenly becomes unaffordable, well, we've

00:07:34.960 --> 00:07:39.520
seen what can happen.

00:08:02.960 --> 00:08:07.199
So governments are under

00:08:05.199 --> 00:08:09.759
pressure, and many are scrambling to

00:08:07.199 --> 00:08:11.840
figure out how to respond right now. But

00:08:09.759 --> 00:08:14.080
those global issues we talked about that

00:08:11.840 --> 00:08:15.840
are pushing food prices up, they don't

00:08:14.080 --> 00:08:18.560
look like they're going away anytime

00:08:15.840 --> 00:08:22.000
soon, and a lot of people all around the

00:08:18.560 --> 00:08:23.919
world are really feeling it.

